  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Write $81{y}^{4}+18{y}^{2}+1$ in factored form as $(a+bi)^{2}(a-bi)^{2}.$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$81{y}^{4}+18{y}^{2}+1=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${y}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{y}^{4}+18{y}^{2}+1 & ={(9{y}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot (9{y}^{2})\\cdot 1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={(9{y}^{2}+1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}+1 & =9{y}^{2}-(-1) \\\\  & =9{y}^{2}-({i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={(3y)}^{2}-{(i)}^{2} \\\\  & =(3y+i)(3y-i)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{y}^{4}+18{y}^{2}+1 & ={(9{y}^{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((3y+i)(3y-i))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(3y+i)}^{2}{(3y-i)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
